  "result": "The command now prints **27.06** (it printed 26.09 before).\n\n**Root cause:** in `shop/money.py`, `to_cents` padded the decimal digits on the left instead of the right. `\"5\".rjust(2, \"0\")` gives `\"05\"`, so `\"12.5\"` was read as 1205 cents instead of 1250. That made the quote 2410 + 199 tax = 26.09 instead of 2500 + 206 tax = 27.06. Prices with two decimals (like `12.05`) were already converted correctly, which is why only one-decimal prices came out wrong.\n\n**Fix:** I changed `rjust` to `ljust` in `to_cents`.\n\n**Tests:** pytest isn't installed, so I ran the suite with `python3 -m unittest`. All 8 tests pass: the 6 existing ones plus 2 I added in `tests/test_money.py`. None of the existing tests covered `to_cents`. The new ones check `12`, `12.5`, `12.05` and `12.50`, and that `1.234` is rejected.\n\n**Separate bug, not fixed:** `to_cents` also gets negative amounts wrong. `\"-1.5\"` gives -50 instead of -150, because the fractional part is added to the negative whole number rather than subtracted. The quote command probably never sees negative prices, but I can fix it if you want.",
